## 4. (HISTORICAL) `S07` could not be calibrated with `M9`, and what the early/late test does and does not say

`S07` with `M9` (`terminated` is delivered before the subject's final `post-stop`) is the one intermittent defect measured.

| Campaign | Protocol | `d` / 200 | Detections at run (position in its JVM) |
|---|---|---|---|
| first calibration | 200 runs in one JVM | 3 | 6, 9, 42 |
| diagnostic replicate | 200 runs in one JVM | 5 | 6, 13, 14, 33, 36 |
| pre-registered (section 7) | **one fresh JVM per run** | **0** | none (200 of 200 judgeable, all exits 0) |

- Fresh JVM per run: `d = 0`. By `docs/judge.md` section 5 item 3 a campaign with no detection is a **harness error**, not a
  survival: the scenario cannot see this defect class under that protocol, and no `N` is chosen (the scenario must change first).
- Runs in one JVM: all 8 detections in 400 runs fall between positions 6 and 42 of their JVM, none in positions 1-5 and
  none in the 316 runs after 42. The hypothesis that fits both facts is that the race window opens only once the JVM is
  warm and closes again later. It is a hypothesis; nothing here tests it.
- So neither protocol gives a `q_low` that a pull request could rely on, and `S07` stays `pending`. Discarding the warm-up
  would remove exactly the window where `M9` is seen, which is why it was not done.

**What the early/late test is.** It compares the detection rate of the first 40 runs of a campaign with the rest (Fisher
exact; level 0.01 divided by the number of campaigns compared). A difference **refutes** a rate that does not depend on the
position of a run, and the row stays `pending`. A pass does **not** show independence or interchangeability: it only
means this one pattern was not detected, with the power the counts allow (a campaign with `d` near `n` has almost none).
The 40-run split was chosen after `S07` showed the effect, so for that scenario it is a diagnostic; for campaigns whose
protocol was fixed beforehand (section 7) it is the gate they were told they would face.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[calibrate.py run: one campaign per mutant] --> B{all 200 runs judgeable?<br/>outcome failed, d >= 1}
    B -- no: timeout, harness-error,<br/>incomplete, d=0, partial --> P[row stays pending<br/>record exits 1]
    B -- yes --> C{rate depends on the run position?<br/>early vs rest, refuted at corrected alpha}
    C -- refuted --> P
    C -- not refuted --> R[row written: d, q_low, N, runs_on_pr<br/>no claim of independence]
```

## 5. Reproduce, and what the job guarantees

```
python3 -I judge/calibrate.py choose
python3 -I judge/calibrate.py run <pekko-copy> <workdir> [S..]          # needs a clean judge/ tree
python3 -I judge/calibrate.py record <workdir> [--also <replicate-dir>]... [--out FILE]
```

- `run` refuses a dirty `judge/` tree, a Pekko copy whose `actor/` or `testkit/` differ from the pin, and the harness test
  knobs. Each campaign writes `manifest.json` with status `started`, then `finished` after judging; a `started`
  directory is a partial execution and is never reused or mixed (`--discard-partial` moves it aside).
- Each manifest records the inputs of the run: Pekko commit, patch hash, harness commit, Java, `sbt`, OS, CPUs, run count,
  deadline, the `sbt.log` hash and, per scenario, the counts, the outcome and one letter per run in run order.
- `record` writes a row only for a campaign whose runs were all judgeable and whose detection rate was not shown to depend on the run position, refuses to mix campaigns
  made under different Pekko, harness bytes, tools, CPUs or run count, and exits non-zero otherwise.
- `.github/workflows/judge-calibration.yml` is manual (`workflow_dispatch`), runs the same commands, never commits, and
  uploads logs, manifests and the candidate table as an artifact **even when it fails**. It was linted with `actionlint`;
  it has **not** been run on GitHub in this work. Rows from a different machine would carry its own CPU count.

