### The causal chains, from the pinned Pekko

Messages are `Msg` values run by the receiving actor; "hop" means a message an actor sends itself while handling the
previous one. File references are in `upstream/pekko/actor/src/main/scala/org/apache/pekko/` (`$A`).

**S18, `I1.b` (new scenario).** Why not `S07`: the wait in `postStop` lets `S07`'s watcher handle `X` before any `Terminated`
exists, which removed the window `M8` needs: `M8/S07` was 7 of 30 before and 0 of 200 with the wait (exploratory run, not a
result). So the wait lives in `S18`, and `S07` is as it was.
1. `w` watches `c` and, in the same handler, sends `c` `probe1`: the `Watch` is in `c`'s system queue before `probe1`.
2. `c` forwards the probe to itself (`probe2`), and only `probe2` completes the promise the driver awaits. A system message is
   drained after the user message being handled at the latest (`$A/dispatch/Mailbox.scala:276`), so the `Watch` was
   processed before `probe2` ran. Only then the driver sends `go`; "`watch` was sent" is not the confirmation used.
3. `c` stops; in `postStop`, after `post-stop-begin`, `PostStopGate` sends `w` three hops (`gate0 -> gate1 -> gate2`) and waits.
   `w` converts a `DeathWatchNotification` into a `Terminated` at the tail of its mailbox in its system processing, after the
   user message in hand (`$A/actor/dungeon/DeathWatch.scala:99-115`). `gate1` is enqueued before that conversion and `gate2`
   after it, so when `gate2` runs any notification that was in `w`'s system queue at `gate0` has been handled.
4. Under `M9` (`tellWatchersWeDied()` before `postStop`) the notification was queued before `gate0`, so `w` records `terminated`
   before `c` records `post-stop`: `I1.b` fires. Under baseline no notification exists yet; the gate releases by itself and
   `terminated` is recorded after `post-stop`. The release never waits for a `Terminated`, so there is no deadlock; the
   5 s `guard` only protects the run, and reaching it records an event outside the vocabulary (a harness error, never a pass
   or a detection).
5. The property in baseline is proved by two recorded events and their global stamps, not by the wait.

**S08, `G6.b`.** `settle(c)` returns the future of a probe that watches `c`; its `Terminated` arrives after `c`'s mailbox was
swapped and cleaned (`$A/actor/dungeon/FaultHandling.scala:261-266`), so `w` watches an already-stopped actor. The `Watch`
goes to the dead-letter mailbox and `DeadLetterActorRef` sends the `DeathWatchNotification` synchronously on `w`'s own thread
(`$A/actor/ActorRef.scala:745-751`). In the same handler `w` sends itself `fin1`; after the handler the system queue is
drained (the notification becomes a `Terminated` behind `fin1`); `fin1` sends `fin`, which is behind that `Terminated`. `fin`
ends the run in both outcomes. `ack1 -> ack2` between two ordinary messages would not have shown this: the proof is that the
notification was already in `w`'s system queue when the hops started. Under `M11` no notification is sent, the log is complete
and `G6.b` fires ("w never saw terminated(c)"): 30/30 and 1/1. Limit: this rests on the system-queue drain after each
message; a baseline where the `Terminated` arrived after `fin` would fail `G6.b` (0 of 230 baseline runs).

**S11, `I3.a`/`I3.b`.** `M5` was sent right after the stop request, racing with the mailbox swap; the milestone table says "after
`terminated` is observed". Now `M5` is sent only after `settle(c)` (termination and clean-up done), so its publication is
synchronous on the driver's thread (`$A/dispatch/Dispatcher.scala:73-77`, `Mailboxes.scala:68-72`, `ActorRef.scala:742`).
The run ends on `rec.sent`, incremented after the `!` returned; the dead-letter subscriber barrier of `runOne` then covers
delivery. `postStop` is not the witness (it runs before the mailbox clean-up, `FaultHandling.scala:254` against `:261`).
`M6` (no publication) now ends the run complete: controlled gives `I3.a`; concurrent gives `REQ` (the required dead letter is
missing) and not `I3.a`, which `S11` asserts only in controlled mode, so that concurrent row is a valid `failed` with
`expected_hit = false`. Because `I3.b` holds in both modes, `S11` is calibrated with `M62` (every dead letter twice): 30/30,
1/1, rule `I3.b`.

**S14, `G7.b`.** The run ends on `terminated` at `w` (Stop) or on a second `pre-start` of the child (the restart that `M14`
causes). A failed initialization leaves no actor, so Pekko does not call `preRestart` (`FaultHandling.scala:94-98`) and the
rule's "restarted" check cannot fire; `M14` is detected because the log is complete, the second `pre-start` is there and the
`Terminated` is not (rules `G7.b` and `REQ`, 30/30 and 1/1). Only the first initialization fails, so the restart does not loop.

**S03 and S17 (`M61`, `G8.a`).** `M61` makes the ask machinery hand an ask the reply of the previous ask of the same system
(replies crossed); the test actor's reply is untouched. The first ask of a system is left alone because the logger start-up
asks internally. Both scenarios detect it in every run (30/30, 1/1 each). They observe different paths: `S03` sees ask,
`received`, `ask-replied`; `S17` sees the same plus a dead letter for the second reply, i.e. also the branch of
`PromiseActorRef.!` that handles a reply after completion (`$A/pattern/AskSupport.scala:608-628`). `M37` is visible only to
`S17`. That `S17` detects `M61` as often as `S03` is **not** a reason to retire `S03`'s concurrent mode, and it was not
retired: its coverage is argued by path, not demonstrated by a mutant that only `S03` could see, and `S03` has its own
calibrated row (`M61`). A proposal to retire it needs the full coverage argument reviewed by a person; none is made here.

